<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abazins</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abazins are a north-west Caucasian people closely related to the Abkhaz, living mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachay-cherkessia">Karachay-Cherkessia</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Their language belongs to the Abkhaz–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Adyghe</a> family. From the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> they lived under the influence of the Kabardian princes, and many emigrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> after the Russian conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abazins.jpg" alt="Abazins" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osipov Georgiy Nokka · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Proposed_Naval_Jack_of_the_Republic_of_Abkhazia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
